1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,780
Tomorrow morning,

2
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:01,950
the Sun sneezes.

3
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:03,190
Right at us.

4
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,770
The biggest solar storm ever recorded,

5
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:07,710
hitting a planet that runs on electricity.

6
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:09,380
What breaks first?

7
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:10,620
How long does it last?

8
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:12,040
And should you actually be worried?

9
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:13,740
Let's find out,

10
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:15,340
starting with the scary part:

11
00:00:15,470 --> 00:00:16,720
how much warning we get.

12
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:18,670
What if the biggest solar storm

13
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,140
in history hit Earth tomorrow?

14
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:23,270
When the Sun throws a giant

15
00:00:23,270 --> 00:00:24,990
cloud of charged gas at us,

16
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:25,950
it has to cross ninety-three

17
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:27,440
million miles of space.

18
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:29,260
The record holder,

19
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,720
back in 1859,

20
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:33,070
made that trip in about seventeen hours.

21
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:36,160
Our last alarm is a satellite a million miles out,

22
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:37,620
parked between us and the Sun,

23
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:38,740
like a doorbell camera.

24
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:40,880
When the cloud hits it,

25
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:42,040
we get somewhere between fifteen

26
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:43,230
minutes and an hour.

27
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:45,640
That's less time than a pizza delivery.

28
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:47,140
And the pizza isn't trying

29
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,430
to fry the power grid.

30
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:50,750
And yes,

31
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:51,910
this has happened before.

32
00:00:52,470 --> 00:00:54,260
September 1859.

33
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:56,670
An astronomer named Richard Carrington sees

34
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:58,240
a white flash on the Sun.

35
00:00:58,830 --> 00:00:59,470
The next night,

36
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:00,880
the sky explodes with color.

37
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:02,840
People in the Caribbean see the northern lights.

38
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,610
And the internet of 1859,

39
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:06,330
the telegraph,

40
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,850
goes completely haywire.

41
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,070
Telegraph machines throw sparks.

42
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:12,080
One operator in Washington gets

43
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:13,430
zapped right on the forehead.

44
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:15,800
Some operators unplug their batteries

45
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,400
and keep sending messages anyway,

46
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:19,170
powered by the storm itself.

47
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:20,900
Free electricity from space.

48
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:22,210
Sounds great.

49
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,490
Until you remember that in 1859,

50
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:26,050
the most advanced thing plugged

51
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:27,400
in was a telegraph.

52
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:28,160
Today,

53
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:28,790
it's everything.

54
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:30,910
So,

55
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:31,380
tomorrow.

56
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:33,220
The first things to go are up in space.

57
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:34,760
In 2022,

58
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:36,600
a storm that scientists called

59
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,880
minor knocked thirty-eight brand-new Starlink

60
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:40,290
satellites out of the sky.

61
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:41,610
A minor one.

62
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:43,050
Then there is GPS.

63
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:45,040
In May 2024,

64
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:47,950
a strong storm threw GPS-guided tractors

65
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,260
in North Dakota off by about ten feet.

66
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:52,630
Some farmers just stopped planting.

67
00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:53,730
Tomorrow,

68
00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:54,850
your maps app tells you,

69
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:55,830
with total confidence,

70
00:01:55,830 --> 00:01:57,600
to turn left into a lake.

71
00:01:58,140 --> 00:01:59,130
Planes reroute.

72
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,690
Radio turns to static.

73
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,310
But your phone still works.

74
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:03,210
For now.

75
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,360
Because the real danger is down here,

76
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:07,550
in the power lines.

77
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:10,250
The storm shakes Earth's magnetic field,

78
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:11,360
and that shoves extra current

79
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:12,480
into long power lines,

80
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:13,520
like a flood into pipes

81
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:14,770
that are way too small.

82
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:17,480
In March 1989,

83
00:02:17,690 --> 00:02:19,340
a much weaker storm hit Quebec.

84
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,250
The whole grid collapsed in about ninety seconds.

85
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:25,390
Six million people lost power for nine hours.

86
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:27,590
And tomorrow's storm is the big one.

87
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:31,330
Here is the part that keeps engineers up at night:

88
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:32,450
the giant transformers.

89
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,120
Each one is about the size of a house,

90
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:35,880
built to order,

91
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:37,590
and a new one can take years to arrive.

92
00:02:38,610 --> 00:02:40,070
The insurer Lloyd's ran the numbers

93
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:42,220
for a storm like 1859.

94
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:44,810
Twenty to forty million Americans without power.

95
00:02:44,810 --> 00:02:45,890
In the worst places,

96
00:02:45,890 --> 00:02:47,260
for up to a year or two.

97
00:02:48,230 --> 00:02:48,490
The bill:

98
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:51,073
up to 2.6 trillion dollars.

99
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:52,910
Your fridge would be the first to know.

100
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:54,040
Then your nose.

101
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:55,940
So,

102
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:56,830
should you be worried?

103
00:02:57,340 --> 00:02:58,610
For a storm this big,

104
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,520
scientists put the odds anywhere from under

105
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:02,440
one percent to about twelve percent,

106
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,370
per decade.

107
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:05,250
And we already had a near miss.

108
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:06,950
In July 2012,

109
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:08,990
the Sun fired a storm just as big.

110
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,660
Had it launched about a week earlier,

111
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:11,430
it would have hit us.

112
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:13,220
The good news:

113
00:03:13,270 --> 00:03:15,240
even an hour of warning is useful.

114
00:03:15,590 --> 00:03:17,150
Grid operators can lower the load,

115
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:19,440
and satellites can hide in safe mode.

116
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:20,660
And for you?

117
00:03:20,850 --> 00:03:21,520
A flashlight,

118
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:22,520
some water,

119
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:23,480
and a paper map.

120
00:03:23,770 --> 00:03:25,320
Old school beats no school.

121
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:28,160
And here's the weird upside.

122
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:29,750
Everyone on Earth gets to

123
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:30,860
see the northern lights.

124
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:31,660
Even in Miami.

125
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:34,440
You'd finally get the perfect selfie.

126
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:36,090
You just couldn't post it.

127
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:37,800
Want to see what happens when

128
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,530
the internet loses its ocean cables?

129
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:40,630
It's right here.

